Here's a C++ function that converts markdown formatted text to html formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <sstream>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html;
    std::istringstream iss(markdown);
    std::string line;
    while (std::getline(iss, line)) {
        html += "<p>" + line + "</p>";
    }
    return html;
}
```

This function takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing html formatted text. It uses a `std::istringstream` to read the markdown text line by line, and then wraps up each line in an HTML `<p>` element.

Here's an example usage of this function:

```cpp
int main() {
    std::string markdown = "# My Title\n\nThis is the first line.\nThis is the second line.";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This will output:

```
<p># My Title<p><p>This is the first line.</p><p>This is the second line.</p>
```

